  "parentCard": {
    "title": "Sprout's third showcase, produced and run by an older sibling, or a grown-up",
    "minutes": 15,
    "includes": "read-aloud",
    "steps": [
      "The older sibling or grown-up sets the room before the star comes in: the family in a row as a real audience, their spot on the floor where everyone can see them, and their things counted out and waiting. Grown-ups sit down and stay down.",
      "Act one, the counting. They fill a ten and say ten, count the extras on top, then fill the second ten and say twenty as two whole tens. Their own speed, and a wobble in the middle is part of the act.",
      "Act two, the two stories. Some toys arrive and they say how many now. Then some toys leave and they say how many are left. Ask how they know, once, and then wait however long the waiting takes.",
      "Act three, the station. They invent a rule and the whole audience has to copy it, then they break their own rule on purpose and catch everybody. They are the rule maker and nobody argues with the maker.",
      "Act four, the close. Their drawn page goes into the Field Atlas beside their sibling's, and the older sibling or grown-up sits down with them in front of everybody and reads a picture book aloud, all the way to the end."
    ],
    "notice": [
      "Do they say twenty as two whole tens, or as a word they have learned by heart?",
      "In the take-away story, do they count what is left from one, or do they already know it?",
      "Who do they look at when the whole family copies their rule?"
    ]
  }
}
